WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.070
My exposure to this entire area of biology began as a medical 

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:07.450
student in my first year at Harvard Medical School.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:12.290
And George Palade's discoveries were really fresh,

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:14.950
and they were presented in my histology course.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:20.370
And when I saw those electron micrographs of vesicles and endoplasmic reticulum and 

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:21.040
Golgi,

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.490
and so on, I immediately started thinking,

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:26.750
given my background in physical sciences,

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.100
how in the world could such a vesicle form?

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.750
You know, what would make that happen?

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.210
How would it know where to fuse?

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:33.800
And I must say,

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.660
somehow I identified that problem as one that I wanted to solve at some point.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:41.550
And I got fixated on it. And I can't really tell you why,

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:43.070
but I just got fixated.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.500
I found it as fascinating then as I do now.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:51.230
So what we did was we wanted to reconstitute transport from the ER to the Golgi.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:52.720
So we took the cells, living,

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:58.060
we gave them a very short pulse label with radioactive methionine and,

00:00:58.070 --> 00:00:58.480
you know,

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:03.090
tricks I had learned in Harvey Lodish's lab, then homogenized the cells 

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:06.200
to retain them in the G protein in the endoplasmic reticulum.

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:12.090
And then we incubated that extract to see if the glycoprotein could be further processed 

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:14.190
by adding certain sugar residues,

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:15.850
especially N-acetyl glucosamine,

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.850
which we knew from the work of Phil Robins and Stuart Kornfeld and others, 

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:22.700
occurred in the Golgi.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.220
So if we could see that processing in vitro,

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.810
it suggested-- it would have suggested-- that transport occur.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:29.080
And indeed,

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:30.330
after I don't know,

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:34.370
a month or two, we observed that that was the case,

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:35.010
we could see it.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:40.070
But then we had a huge background. Even without any kind of an incubation 

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.450
of longer than a few seconds,

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.030
even without adding ATP or cytosol,

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:46.600
we still saw processing.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.370
And what we came to realize was that we already had too much of the G protein sitting in the Golgi,

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.820
and it was getting glycosylated directly there.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:58.660
So that gave me the idea of what is now known as a complementation assay,

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:02.760
which is pretty much the standard design for most-- in those days--

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:07.560
most cell-free reconstitutions of transport. And what the idea is, is you want to mix two 

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.180
extracts,

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.890
one from a cell that contains the cargo protein, VSV G,

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:16.520
but lacks the processing enzyme. The other extract from a cell that 

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:20.410
has the processing enzyme, but lacks the cargo protein.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:22.140
And so when the two are mixed,

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:25.620
if the cargo protein is getting glycosylated,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:30.120
it must have gone from a donor membrane in the extract 

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:34.810
of the one cell, to the processing enzyme in the Gogli of the other cell.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:39.710
And so that eliminated the background that I referred to because the extract with the 

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:44.000
VSV G protein was incapable of processing by adding sugar.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.170
So when we set that assay up, it actually worked.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.470
It was in I believe 1979.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:51.510
I still have the gel.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:52.160
It shows a very,

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:57.090
very faint band of VSV G protein being processed to what's called an endo H 

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:58.250
resistant form.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:02:59.420
It was very,

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:00.150
very exciting.

